"comparison": "Both Intel and Micron identify AI capabilities as a driver for new PC hardware requirements. Intel noted that the Intel Core Ultra Series 1 was the 'first AI PCs to the market,' highlighting the CPU's NPU. Micron corroborated the market shift by stating that 'next generation PCs contain high-performance neural processing chipsets as well as AI capabilities and require higher performance and higher average capacity SSDs than traditional PCs.' Intel's disclosure focuses on the CPU/NPU component driving the AI PC classification, while Micron's disclosure explains the storage implications (higher performance SSDs) resulting from these AI-capable chipsets.",
